WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.049
Hello, and welcome to this video.

2
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:07.089
We are going to be doing the first exercise.

3
00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:12.369
So we will be on slide 7 in the top right-hand corner,

4
00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:14.769
and you should hopefully see this little scene here

5
00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:18.850
where we have a number of different meshes

6
00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.449
that are all in a sort of silver.

7
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.550
Now, what we're going to be doing here is

8
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.350
we're going to be looking at how you can use

9
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:32.350
Material Instance in order to control effects.

10
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.869
So this is sort of just a very, very short exercise.

11
00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:40.030
And we're just going to be looking at these materials.

12
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.750
So you should hopefully have the material shown

13
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:47.070
in the Content Browser.

14
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.750
If we double-click and open that one up here,

15
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:51.950
you can see that we've got something

16
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:53.789
that looks a little bit like this.

17
00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.509
Now, this has actually got an effect that's part of it.

18
00:00:58.509 --> 00:01:01.229
What we're doing is we're using the bounding box,

19
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:10.750
and we are using that in order to control the effect.

20
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.750
Now you can see here we have the subtract value,

21
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.870
and then we are plugging something into the Emissive

22
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.189
and the Opacity Mask.

23
00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:22.590
So if I was to take this fade amount,

24
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.790
change it to 0.5,

25
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:31.870
you can see that the mesh has gone halfway down it.

26
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.150
It's got this sort of glowing outer ring.

27
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.430
If I was to apply this and jump back into the level,

28
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.670
you'd see that all of the objects that we have

29
00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.469
that are using this material also have now lost their top halves.

30
00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.209
So it's this fade amount value here,

31
00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:51.489
which is controlling it.

32
00:01:51.489 --> 00:01:55.090
So 0.5 would mean 50% of the way through.

33
00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.209
It would do 0.9.

34
00:01:56.209 --> 00:01:58.049
It would mean that 90% of the way through.

35
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.840
But we want to be able to control this within the gauge.

36
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.400
And the first step for that is,

37
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.840
you can change that back to zero,

38
00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:11.150
is to make sure that this is a parameter.

39
00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:14.819
What a parameter means is it means

40
00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:17.780
that we're going to be able to control this externally.

41
00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:21.699
I'm going to call this parameter Fade Amount,

42
00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:26.509
and I will apply and save.

43
00:02:28.879 --> 00:02:30.319
Now you'll see next to this,

44
00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:33.840
this we have a material instance for the same material.

45
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.639
And that's what is applied to these different meshes.

46
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.419
If I was to open this up now and drag this across

47
00:02:42.419 --> 00:02:43.300
so that we can see,

48
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.159
we can now see we have the fade amount material

49
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.919
that we created is an option to be updated here.

50
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:54.639
And if I was to push tick,

51
00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.639
you would now see as I drag this,

52
00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:02.479
the effect runs through all of the different meshes.

53
00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:08.659
So everything that is owning that material instance

54
00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:12.979
is now controllable by this variable.

55
00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:16.180
Now that's relatively straightforward,

56
00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.539
but we're going to look later

57
00:03:17.539 --> 00:03:19.699
how we can change that programmatically.

58
00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.900
And again, because we've used the bounding box,

59
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:28.020
this means that this material will work on any mesh

60
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.460
that it is assigned to.

61
00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:35.460
Great, so that is the complete of the exercise.

62
00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:40.340
Next, we will look at doing this programmatically at runtime.
